; Parse failure, alone. The corpus had no case containing a syntax error at
; all, so "trees always exist after parse (possibly partial) — collection
; over abort" had never crossed a host boundary: a host was free to abort on
; a parse error and no gate would notice. One did — see
; `hosts/typescript-parity/src/parser.ts`.
;
; `(pt :x 1` is unclosed at end of input. `Parser.closeUnclosedFrames`
; finalises the frame at `source.len`, so the recovered tree must hold a
; *complete* `(pt :x 1)`: `:x` is required (keys are required unless
; `:optional true`), so a host that recovered an empty or truncated form
; would add `missing_required_key` here. Exactly one diagnostic is the
; assertion that the child was really attached.
(plugin :name p :version "1.0.0"
  (form :name pt (key :name x :type number)))

(pt :x 1
